  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 919 AM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nA weak upper-level ridge is stationed over New Mexico as prevailing\nzonal flow moves across the northern tier of the United States\ntoday. The ridge is also keeping deep subtropical moisture\nconfided to Mexico, with the region only seeing clouds for today.\nEasterly flow from a surface high in the Southern Plains will\nbring east winds up to 15 mph and keep temperatures for the region\nin the mid-to-upper 70s today.\n\nHeading into tomorrow, the ridge moves out as a Pacific trough\nenters the Great Basin. The trough will raise temperatures into\nthe upper 80s, close to normal for early October. Dewpoint\ntemperatures in the upper 40s/low 50s will aid with the\ndevelopment of high clouds, but little to no precipitation\nexpected.\n\nThe trough enters New Mexico on Wednesday and drops into Texas on\nThursday, which should increase PWAT values to 0.7 inches in the\nmountains and 0.8 inches down south. A very slight chance of\nshowers in the mountains are possible for both days, with the\nlowlands remaining mostly dry.\n\nThe trough moves out of the area on Friday. Into the weekend, a \nweak shortwave trough brings some moisture back to the region, \nwith a chance for showers possible out west on Sunday. Further\nout, the models are conflicted for early next week's weather, with\nthe GFS having a ridge over the region, and the ECMWF having a\nlong Pacific trough enter the region.",
